nodecode-osint-map is a rewrite of Adolanium's Adolanium/hermes-osint-map:

  https://github.com/Adolanium/hermes-osint-map
  commit 8254b9e872c2718e5ea228168db3f75fb77955de
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Adolanium (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the five tools keep their names, descriptions and parameters
(osint_situation, osint_events, osint_country, osint_recon, osint_news), and
they answer from the same public, keyless sources — USGS earthquakes, NASA
EONET, GDACS disaster alerts, US National Weather Service alerts, adsb.lol
military aircraft, NOAA SWPC's planetary K index, BBC/Al Jazeera/DW/The
Guardian/France 24 RSS plus GDELT's article search, the World Bank, CoinGecko
and an optional Twelve Data key. The recon lookups keep their kinds (dns,
rdap, subdomains, cve with CISA KEV, ip, github, mac), their targets' checks
and the same endpoints (Google Public DNS, rdap.org, crt.sh, CIRCL, the CISA
KEV feed, ipwho.is, api.github.com, MacVendors). The item shape, the severity
scale of each layer, the per-source cache and the "a source that fails says so
in its own line" behaviour are carried over, as is the optional Twelve Data
key, which this cell reads from the environment variable the section names.

What was left out, and why: the Desktop page (a three.js globe with SGP4
satellite propagation, the traffic-camera stills by operator id, maritime
chokepoints and per-layer map widgets) is a UI no agent tool answered from, so
it is not here; the incidents layer is gone because it read GDELT's zipped
export, and the events tool answers from the five layers the original's own
tools listed; the tls and headers recon kinds are gone because they open a TLS
or HTTP socket by hand, and this cell reaches every source through one HTTP
seam; and SWPC's R/S/G scales and alert feed are trimmed to the Kp index the
situation brief quotes. LICENSE is the original repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
